WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:10.866
Design equity requires language precise enough to describe unequal conditions without turning a difficult conversation into a contest over personal character.

2
00:00:10.866 --> 00:00:20.932
Racism can operate through rules, institutions, accumulated investment, and routine professional practice, including when no participant states a discriminatory intent.

3
00:00:20.932 --> 00:00:28.781
This episode adapts Kristine Miller's chapter Learning to Talk about Racism from Introduction to Design Equity, licensed CC BY 4.0.

4
00:00:28.781 --> 00:00:31.477
It paraphrases and reorganizes Miller's text.

5
00:00:31.477 --> 00:00:36.150
Third-party readings, videos, images, quotations, and statistics are excluded.

6
00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:40.338
Begin by separating four things: intent, action, pattern, and outcome.

7
00:00:40.338 --> 00:00:42.612
Intent asks what a person hoped to do.

8
00:00:42.612 --> 00:00:45.423
Action asks what the decision actually changed.

9
00:00:45.423 --> 00:00:50.688
Pattern asks whether similar decisions repeatedly advantage or burden particular groups.

10
00:00:50.688 --> 00:00:55.056
Outcome asks who receives access, safety, wealth, exposure, or exclusion.

11
00:00:55.056 --> 00:00:58.825
Intent matters, but it cannot answer the other three questions.

12
00:00:58.825 --> 00:01:00.560
Use specific nouns and verbs.

13
00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:06.364
Say which policy, boundary, investment, design standard, or engagement rule is under examination.

14
00:01:06.364 --> 00:01:07.800
Say what evidence shows.

15
00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:15.218
Avoid using a whole community as a symbol, and avoid expecting one participant to speak for everyone who shares an identity.

16
00:01:15.218 --> 00:01:17.312
Discomfort is not the same as harm.

17
00:01:17.312 --> 00:01:22.996
A productive process can acknowledge discomfort while keeping attention on the public decision.

18
00:01:22.996 --> 00:01:29.338
Ground rules should protect participation without preventing evidence-based discussion of race and racism.

19
00:01:29.338 --> 00:01:32.704
An equity discussion needs more than aggregate averages.

20
00:01:32.704 --> 00:01:40.157
Ask how results vary by race together with income, disability, age, tenure, language, and location when reliable data allow.

21
00:01:40.157 --> 00:01:43.342
Small numbers require privacy protection and caution.

22
00:01:43.342 --> 00:01:47.910
A disparity is a prompt for investigation, not automatic proof of one cause.

23
00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:49.473
Create an evidence ladder.

24
00:01:49.473 --> 00:01:51.517
Start with the observed condition.

25
00:01:51.517 --> 00:01:53.560
Add historical and policy context.

26
00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:54.943
Add community accounts.

27
00:01:54.943 --> 00:01:56.746
Identify plausible mechanisms.

28
00:01:56.746 --> 00:01:57.828
Test alternatives.

29
00:01:57.828 --> 00:01:59.751
Then state what remains unknown.

30
00:01:59.751 --> 00:02:04.980
This keeps the conversation analytical and prevents certainty from outrunning evidence.

31
00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:06.616
Torrance application segment.

32
00:02:06.616 --> 00:02:10.227
This is original local analysis, separate from Miller's chapter.

33
00:02:10.227 --> 00:02:16.546
The cited source is the official City of Torrance General Plan page, checked October seventh, twenty twenty-six.

34
00:02:16.546 --> 00:02:20.495
Imagine reviewing outreach for the Land Use and Safety Element update.

35
00:02:20.495 --> 00:02:23.767
Do not ask only whether everyone received the same notice.

36
00:02:23.767 --> 00:02:30.199
Ask whether language, schedule, location, disability access, digital access, and trust affected who could respond.

37
00:02:30.199 --> 00:02:35.841
Compare participation with the population affected, but do not reduce representativeness to a quota.

38
00:02:35.841 --> 00:02:41.426
Publish the outreach methods, summarize themes without erasing disagreement, and show what changed.

39
00:02:41.426 --> 00:02:46.560
If a racial pattern appears, describe it accurately and examine possible policy mechanisms.

40
00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:48.760
Do not assign motives without evidence.

41
00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:51.807
Do not claim the exercise determines legal compliance.

42
00:02:51.807 --> 00:02:55.587
Current law, facts, and qualified advice control any real decision.

43
00:02:55.587 --> 00:02:57.799
The goal is not perfect vocabulary.

44
00:02:57.799 --> 00:03:07.786
It is a disciplined public practice: name the decision, distinguish intent from impact, examine patterns, protect participants, and remain open to correction.

45
00:03:07.786 --> 00:03:10.947
This CC BY 4.0 adaptation credits Kristine Miller.

46
00:03:10.947 --> 00:03:16.699
It excludes all third-party chapter items and adds a separately cited Torrance application.

47
00:03:16.699 --> 00:03:18.342
No endorsement is implied.
